1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,355
Albert Einstein built modern physics on one unbreakable

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:07,830
rule: nothing in the universe can travel faster

3
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,510
than the speed of light.

4
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:12,130
Not a rocket, not a radio signal, not

5
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:14,860
even a single subatomic particle.

6
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:18,370
Yet right now, the James Webb Space Telescope

7
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:22,030
is staring straight at a real galaxy known

8
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:29,030
as JADES-GS-z14-0 that is racing away from Earth

9
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,550
at more than twice the speed of light.

10
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,420
And it isn't some rare glitch.

11
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:36,310
Most of the galaxies visible in our night

12
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,830
sky are receding faster than light right this

13
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:39,550
second.

14
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:43,020
So how can entire galaxies outrun light without

15
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:45,630
shattering Einstein's most famous law?

16
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:48,380
And even stranger: if they are moving away

17
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:50,700
faster than light, how did their light ever

18
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:53,430
reach our telescopes in the first place?

19
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:56,230
The answer forces you to separate two completely

20
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,470
different kinds of motion and once you see

21
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:01,910
the difference, you'll realize why roughly ninety-five percent

22
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:04,070
of the galaxies we can see tonight are

23
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:06,290
already gone from our reach forever.

24
00:01:07,231 --> 00:01:09,941
To solve the paradox, we have to look

25
00:01:09,791 --> 00:01:13,651
closely at what Einstein's nineteen-oh-five theory of special

26
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:16,661
relativity actually bans.

27
00:01:16,681 --> 00:01:19,571
Picture a spacecraft firing its engines in empty

28
00:01:19,421 --> 00:01:20,401
space.

29
00:01:20,741 --> 00:01:23,827
As it pushes faster and faster toward three

30
00:01:23,961 --> 00:01:27,391
hundred thousand kilometers per second, special relativity says

31
00:01:27,241 --> 00:01:30,541
the energy needed to speed it up climbs

32
00:01:30,391 --> 00:01:32,671
toward infinity.

33
00:01:32,561 --> 00:01:35,121
The fabric of space itself acts like an

34
00:01:35,311 --> 00:01:39,771
unbreakable speed limit for anything traveling through space.

35
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:43,011
So if no engine, explosion, or force can

36
00:01:42,861 --> 00:01:45,171
push a galaxy past the speed of light,

37
00:01:45,651 --> 00:01:49,291
what on Earth is accelerating distant galaxies past

38
00:01:49,141 --> 00:01:50,251
it?

39
00:01:50,141 --> 00:01:53,151
The surprising answer is: nothing is pushing them

40
00:01:53,001 --> 00:01:55,091
through space at all.

41
00:01:55,101 --> 00:01:58,981
Ten years after special relativity, Einstein published general

42
00:01:58,831 --> 00:02:02,611
relativity a far bigger theory where space isn't

43
00:02:02,461 --> 00:02:04,411
a rigid, fixed stage.

44
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:08,861
Space itself can bend, curve, and stretch.

45
00:02:08,781 --> 00:02:12,501
And in nineteen twenty-seven, physicist Georges Lemaître realized

46
00:02:12,351 --> 00:02:16,851
Einstein's equations predicted an expanding universe a prediction

47
00:02:16,701 --> 00:02:19,331
Edwin Hubble confirmed just two years later in

48
00:02:19,404 --> 00:02:23,211
nineteen twenty-nine when he measured distant galaxies drifting

49
00:02:23,061 --> 00:02:25,681
away from us in every direction.

50
00:02:25,621 --> 00:02:29,021
Those galaxies aren't rocketing through space faster than

51
00:02:28,871 --> 00:02:29,361
light.

52
00:02:29,421 --> 00:02:32,601
Instead, the space between us and them is

53
00:02:32,451 --> 00:02:36,701
stretching creating brand-new space every single second.

54
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:41,114
To see how stretching space breaks the light-speed

55
00:02:40,964 --> 00:02:45,444
barrier without breaking Einstein, imagine placing galaxies on

56
00:02:45,294 --> 00:02:48,844
the grid lines of a giant elastic sheet.

57
00:02:48,704 --> 00:02:51,704
Pin our Milky Way to one intersection, and

58
00:02:51,554 --> 00:02:54,214
pin a chain of distant galaxies down the

59
00:02:54,064 --> 00:02:54,774
line.

60
00:02:54,664 --> 00:02:58,074
Notice that every galaxy stays parked right on

61
00:02:57,924 --> 00:02:59,514
its own grid point.

62
00:02:59,744 --> 00:03:02,384
Locally, none of them are racing through space

63
00:03:02,234 --> 00:03:03,284
at all.

64
00:03:03,164 --> 00:03:06,074
Now stretch the grid uniformly.

65
00:03:05,944 --> 00:03:09,184
Every single square grows slightly wider at the

66
00:03:09,034 --> 00:03:13,134
exact same rate roughly sixty-seven kilometers per second

67
00:03:12,984 --> 00:03:16,994
for every three-point-three million light-years of distance.

68
00:03:16,844 --> 00:03:20,124
For a nearby galaxy just one grid square

69
00:03:19,974 --> 00:03:23,024
away, that stretch is tiny nowhere near light

70
00:03:22,874 --> 00:03:23,694
speed.

71
00:03:23,544 --> 00:03:26,844
For a galaxy two squares away, two stretches

72
00:03:26,694 --> 00:03:29,674
add up, so it separates twice as fast.

73
00:03:29,624 --> 00:03:32,504
Three squares away, three times as fast.

74
00:03:32,384 --> 00:03:36,154
That simple pattern is Hubble's Law: the farther

75
00:03:36,004 --> 00:03:38,954
away a galaxy sits, the more stretching space

76
00:03:38,804 --> 00:03:41,544
lies between us, and the faster the gap

77
00:03:41,394 --> 00:03:42,414
grows.

78
00:03:42,264 --> 00:03:45,174
Keep stacking enough grid squares side by side,

79
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:47,834
and eventually the combined expansion adds up to

80
00:03:47,684 --> 00:03:51,394
more than three hundred thousand kilometers per second.

81
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:53,524
You can picture the exact same trick with

82
00:03:53,374 --> 00:03:55,964
an ant walking along a rubber band.

83
00:03:55,824 --> 00:03:58,464
Suppose the ant's absolute top speed is one

84
00:03:58,314 --> 00:04:02,404
centimeter per second its own unbreakable speed limit.

85
00:04:02,264 --> 00:04:04,124
Now pull both ends of the rubber band

86
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:06,684
apart at five centimeters per second.

87
00:04:06,584 --> 00:04:09,454
At every moment, the ant never cheats; its

88
00:04:09,304 --> 00:04:11,964
feet still crawl over the rubber right underneath

89
00:04:11,814 --> 00:04:14,054
it at one centimeter per second.

90
00:04:13,904 --> 00:04:16,014
Yet the opposite end of the rubber band

91
00:04:15,864 --> 00:04:19,184
is receding five times faster than the ant

92
00:04:19,034 --> 00:04:20,054
can walk.

93
00:04:20,214 --> 00:04:23,084
Galaxies obey the exact same rule: nothing ever

94
00:04:23,169 --> 00:04:25,554
outruns a beam of light passing right beside

95
00:04:25,404 --> 00:04:26,374
it locally.

96
00:04:26,264 --> 00:04:29,334
General relativity simply puts no speed limit on

97
00:04:29,184 --> 00:04:31,964
how fast empty space itself can expand.

98
00:04:32,833 --> 00:04:36,563
That cleanly saves Einstein's speed limit but it

99
00:04:36,413 --> 00:04:40,373
immediately leaves us with our second, even stranger

100
00:04:40,223 --> 00:04:41,323
puzzle.

101
00:04:41,193 --> 00:04:43,783
Draw a giant sphere around Earth at a

102
00:04:43,633 --> 00:04:47,023
radius of fourteen-point-four billion light-years.

103
00:04:46,913 --> 00:04:50,023
Astronomers call this boundary the Hubble Sphere.

104
00:04:49,873 --> 00:04:53,703
Right on that line, expanding space carries galaxies

105
00:04:53,553 --> 00:04:56,103
away from us at exactly the speed of

106
00:04:55,953 --> 00:04:56,783
light.

107
00:04:56,633 --> 00:05:01,623
Beyond fourteen-point-four billion light-years, galaxies recede faster than

108
00:05:01,473 --> 00:05:02,263
light.

109
00:05:02,193 --> 00:05:04,913
So pause and ask a natural question: if

110
00:05:04,763 --> 00:05:07,933
a galaxy outside that sphere shines a beam

111
00:05:07,783 --> 00:05:10,943
of light toward Earth today, isn't that light

112
00:05:10,793 --> 00:05:13,903
automatically swept backward forever?

113
00:05:14,268 --> 00:05:16,553
Remarkably, the answer is no and this is

114
00:05:16,403 --> 00:05:19,143
one of the most common misconceptions in all

115
00:05:18,993 --> 00:05:20,573
of astronomy.

116
00:05:20,473 --> 00:05:23,763
Follow a single photon emitted toward Earth from

117
00:05:23,613 --> 00:05:27,223
a galaxy fifteen-point-five billion light-years away.

118
00:05:27,073 --> 00:05:30,103
Because space there is receding faster than light,

119
00:05:29,953 --> 00:05:32,243
the photon loses ground at first.

120
00:05:32,113 --> 00:05:35,143
Even though it travels forward through local space

121
00:05:34,993 --> 00:05:38,493
at full light speed, the expanding fabric drags

122
00:05:38,343 --> 00:05:40,683
it farther away from Earth!

123
00:05:40,553 --> 00:05:43,263
So how can it possibly make it home?

124
00:05:43,113 --> 00:05:46,123
Because the expansion rate of the universe per

125
00:05:45,973 --> 00:05:50,283
unit distance the Hubble parameter is actually decreasing

126
00:05:50,133 --> 00:05:51,193
over time.

127
00:05:51,073 --> 00:05:53,223
As it drops, you have to look farther

128
00:05:53,073 --> 00:05:55,883
out to find space receding at light speed.

129
00:05:55,753 --> 00:05:58,253
In other words, the Hubble Sphere itself is

130
00:05:58,103 --> 00:05:59,803
growing outward!

131
00:05:59,673 --> 00:06:02,453
As the photon keeps swimming upstream at light

132
00:06:02,303 --> 00:06:06,233
speed, the expanding Hubble Sphere eventually overtakes it.

133
00:06:06,113 --> 00:06:09,113
The instant that boundary passes the photon, space

134
00:06:08,963 --> 00:06:12,043
around it is now receding slower than light.

135
00:06:11,913 --> 00:06:15,493
The photon stops drifting backward, starts closing the

136
00:06:15,343 --> 00:06:18,413
distance to Earth, and billions of years later

137
00:06:18,263 --> 00:06:20,153
hits a telescope mirror.

138
00:06:20,033 --> 00:06:22,543
If watching a beam of light literally get

139
00:06:22,393 --> 00:06:25,713
rescued by an expanding cosmic boundary just blew

140
00:06:25,563 --> 00:06:27,763
your mind, take a quick second to hit

141
00:06:27,613 --> 00:06:30,443
that like button it really helps visual physics

142
00:06:30,293 --> 00:06:32,283
explainers reach more curious viewers.

143
00:06:33,085 --> 00:06:34,525
Wait a second.

144
00:06:34,375 --> 00:06:37,305
If the Hubble Sphere keeps growing outward to

145
00:06:37,155 --> 00:06:40,795
rescue photons, does that mean light from any

146
00:06:40,645 --> 00:06:44,025
distance will eventually reach us if we just

147
00:06:43,875 --> 00:06:44,735
wait long enough?

148
00:06:44,955 --> 00:06:49,485
No and to see why, bring back our

149
00:06:49,335 --> 00:06:50,665
ant on the rubber band.

150
00:06:50,715 --> 00:06:53,545
If you stretch a rubber band at a

151
00:06:53,395 --> 00:06:57,675
steady, constant rate, a shocking piece of math

152
00:06:57,525 --> 00:07:00,215
takes over: even if the far end moves

153
00:07:00,065 --> 00:07:02,665
away ten times faster than the ant can

154
00:07:02,515 --> 00:07:05,705
walk, the ant will still eventually reach the

155
00:07:05,555 --> 00:07:09,065
other side, because each step permanently locks in

156
00:07:08,915 --> 00:07:10,655
a tiny fraction of the band.

157
00:07:10,735 --> 00:07:16,025
Now change just one thing: instead of stretching

158
00:07:15,875 --> 00:07:20,305
steadily, accelerate the stretch exponentially so every meter

159
00:07:20,155 --> 00:07:23,025
of rubber doubles in size over a fixed

160
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:24,625
interval of time.

161
00:07:24,475 --> 00:07:27,335
Suddenly, the newly created rubber ahead of the

162
00:07:27,185 --> 00:07:29,865
ant explodes faster than the ant can ever

163
00:07:29,715 --> 00:07:32,105
claim a meaningful fraction of it.

164
00:07:31,955 --> 00:07:34,755
Beyond a sharp cutoff distance, the ant is

165
00:07:34,605 --> 00:07:35,565
trapped forever.

166
00:07:35,615 --> 00:07:40,385
In nineteen ninety-eight, astronomers discovered that our universe

167
00:07:40,235 --> 00:07:42,965
behaves like that second rubber band.

168
00:07:43,070 --> 00:07:45,865
Driven by dark energy, the expansion of space

169
00:07:45,715 --> 00:07:47,765
is accelerating.

170
00:07:47,635 --> 00:07:50,435
Because of dark energy, our Hubble Sphere won't

171
00:07:50,285 --> 00:07:53,095
keep growing forever it will max out around

172
00:07:53,178 --> 00:07:55,615
seventeen-point-five billion light-years.

173
00:07:55,475 --> 00:07:57,735
That locks in a true point of no

174
00:07:57,585 --> 00:08:01,695
return called the Cosmic Event Horizon, sitting roughly

175
00:08:01,723 --> 00:08:04,145
sixteen-point-five billion light-years away today.

176
00:08:03,995 --> 00:08:08,185
A photon leaving a galaxy at fifteen-point-five billion

177
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:11,255
light-years today will still be rescued and reach

178
00:08:11,105 --> 00:08:11,925
Earth.

179
00:08:11,835 --> 00:08:14,665
A photon leaving a galaxy at seventeen billion

180
00:08:14,515 --> 00:08:18,065
light-years today never will no matter how many

181
00:08:17,915 --> 00:08:20,123
trillions of years it travels.

182
00:08:20,823 --> 00:08:25,403
Now everything clicks together into three nested shells

183
00:08:25,253 --> 00:08:26,363
centered on Earth.

184
00:08:26,423 --> 00:08:31,473
First, at fourteen-point-four billion light-years, sits the Hubble

185
00:08:31,323 --> 00:08:34,623
Sphere where recession speed crosses the speed of

186
00:08:34,473 --> 00:08:35,693
light today.

187
00:08:35,783 --> 00:08:40,213
Just outside it, at sixteen-point-five billion light-years, sits

188
00:08:40,063 --> 00:08:43,883
the Cosmic Event Horizon the absolute cutoff for

189
00:08:43,733 --> 00:08:46,833
any light leaving a galaxy right now.

190
00:08:46,888 --> 00:08:50,073
Yet our Observable Universe reaches all the way

191
00:08:49,923 --> 00:08:54,263
out to forty-six-point-five billion light-years in every direction

192
00:08:54,303 --> 00:08:57,163
ninety-three billion light-years across!

193
00:08:57,103 --> 00:09:01,083
How can we see JADES-GS-z14-0, which sits thirty-three-point-eight

194
00:09:00,933 --> 00:09:04,413
billion light-years away right now more than twice

195
00:09:04,263 --> 00:09:07,453
as far as our cosmic event horizon?

196
00:09:07,303 --> 00:09:11,293
Because we aren't seeing light it emitted today.

197
00:09:11,203 --> 00:09:15,013
We are catching ancient light it emitted thirteen-point-five

198
00:09:14,863 --> 00:09:18,093
billion years ago, when the young universe was

199
00:09:17,943 --> 00:09:20,873
tiny and that galaxy sat barely two billion

200
00:09:20,723 --> 00:09:23,273
light-years from our neighborhood.

201
00:09:23,263 --> 00:09:26,113
Its photons were swept up by our expanding

202
00:09:25,963 --> 00:09:29,523
Hubble Sphere early in cosmic history and began

203
00:09:29,373 --> 00:09:31,533
making their way toward us.

204
00:09:31,643 --> 00:09:34,933
During the thirteen-point-five billion years those photons spent

205
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:38,373
crossing space, expansion carried the galaxy itself all

206
00:09:38,223 --> 00:09:42,243
the way out to thirty-three-point-eight billion light-years away.

207
00:09:42,223 --> 00:09:45,213
We are seeing its ancient baby photo even

208
00:09:45,063 --> 00:09:47,903
though the light it is shining right now

209
00:09:47,863 --> 00:09:49,203
will never reach us.

210
00:09:50,273 --> 00:09:52,713
Step back and look at the volume of

211
00:09:52,563 --> 00:09:55,243
those two spheres side by side.

212
00:09:55,113 --> 00:10:00,193
The reachable region inside our sixteen-point-five-billion-light-year event horizon

213
00:10:00,043 --> 00:10:02,043
makes up only about five percent of the

214
00:10:01,893 --> 00:10:05,133
volume of the observable universe.

215
00:10:05,113 --> 00:10:07,533
That means roughly ninety-five percent of all the

216
00:10:07,383 --> 00:10:10,703
galaxies we can see through telescopes tonight are

217
00:10:10,553 --> 00:10:13,493
already permanently beyond our reach.

218
00:10:13,533 --> 00:10:15,273
Even if we launched a beam of light

219
00:10:15,123 --> 00:10:17,633
straight toward them right this second, it would

220
00:10:17,483 --> 00:10:19,453
never arrive.

221
00:10:19,353 --> 00:10:21,783
And over the next hundreds of billions of

222
00:10:21,633 --> 00:10:25,133
years, dark energy will keep carrying distant galaxies

223
00:10:24,983 --> 00:10:28,053
across that event horizon one by one.

224
00:10:27,953 --> 00:10:31,033
Their light will stretch into longer, dimmer red

225
00:10:30,883 --> 00:10:35,063
wavelengths until they fade completely into darkness.

226
00:10:35,193 --> 00:10:37,583
Trillions of years from now, only the galaxies

227
00:10:37,433 --> 00:10:40,073
bound together by gravity our own Milky Way

228
00:10:39,923 --> 00:10:43,223
merged with Andromeda and our closest neighbors will

229
00:10:43,073 --> 00:10:46,193
remain in the sky, surrounded by an empty

230
00:10:46,043 --> 00:10:47,703
black void.

231
00:10:47,653 --> 00:10:50,523
Nothing ever broke Einstein's speed limit.

232
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:53,983
Space simply grew larger than light could ever

233
00:10:54,253 --> 00:10:54,653
outrun.

234
